       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 7,650 mg/day of metformin is three times the maximum daily dose, and I know of no clinical indication for it. The main risk is severe metformin-associated lactic acidosis, which can be fatal. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't remove that risk at this dose.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day and the labeled maximum.\n- **Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **A different dose you choose** at or below 2,550 mg/day.\n\nIf you think this dose is justified, for example by a protocol or a research setting, you could take it through pharmacy and the formal override or dose-verification process. That route puts a second clinician in the loop, and I can't replace that step.\n\nWhich of these should I enter?"
      }
